  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 336 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nFor the better part of next week, ridging will persist across \nthe southwestern CONUS, while a persistent trough of low \npressure remains over eastern Canada into the eastern Great \nLakes and New England. Broad, dry northwesterly flow will \npersist across the central plains through the end of next week. \nLittle to no precipitation is expected in the long term and the \nlatest NBM ensemble forecast has a near zero chance of \nmeasurable precipitation Sunday night through Friday. As for \ntemperatures, a broad thermal ridge will persist over the \nIntermountain West, central and southern plains next week. This \nwill lead to daily highs in the 70s and lower 80s, some 10 to 15\ndegrees above normal for this time of year. Tuesday appears to \nbe the warmest day of the week with readings approaching the \nmiddle 80s over southwestern Nebraska. ATTM, spread in the NBM \nhigh temperature ensembles remains quite low next week and feel \nconfident in the temperature forecast next week. Beyond Friday \ninto next weekend, ridging begins to break down, placing the \ncentral plains in westerly and west-southwesterly flow aloft. \nWith an active northern stream, we may see an uptick in \nprecipitation chances next weekend. However, based on decent \ndifferences between the deterministic EC and GFS solns next \nweekend, forecast confidence with respect to precipitation \nchances remains low ATTM.",
